<h1>Introduction to Apache Cassandra</h1>

<p>Apache Cassandra is a distributed NoSQL database designed to handle large amounts of data across many commodity servers, providing high availability with no single point of failure. It’s known for its scalability, fault-tolerance, and decentralized nature, making it ideal for applications that require high performance, reliability, and scalability.</p>

<h2>Key Features</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Decentralized and Distributed:</strong> Cassandra operates on a peer-to-peer architecture, where each node is equal and contributes to the system's resilience.</li>
    <li><strong>High Scalability:</strong> Designed to handle large amounts of data across multiple servers, Cassandra offers linear scalability with increasing nodes.</li>
    <li><strong>Fault Tolerance:</strong> Data is replicated across multiple nodes, ensuring availability even in case of node failure.</li>
    <li><strong>High Performance:</strong> By allowing high-speed read and write operations, Cassandra can efficiently manage heavy workloads.</li>
    <li><strong>Flexible Schema:</strong> Supports schema-free architecture, making it easier to handle unstructured and semi-structured data.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Architecture Overview</h2>
<p>Cassandra's architecture is decentralized, meaning there is no master node, which allows for continuous availability and fault tolerance.</p>

