<h2>Data Model</h2>
<p>Cassandra’s data model is built around the concept of tables, rows, and columns. It is highly flexible, allowing you to structure data without a rigid schema.</p>

<div class="diagram">
    <h3>Data Distribution and Replication</h3>
    <p>Cassandra replicates data across multiple nodes for redundancy and reliability. The replication factor defines the number of copies of data across the cluster.</p>
    <img src="~/images/cassandra-data-replication.png" alt="Cassandra Data Replication" style="width:100%; max-width:600px;">
</div>

<h2>Getting Started with Cassandra</h2>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Download and Install:</strong> You can download Cassandra from the official website. Follow the installation guide for your operating system.</li>
    <li><strong>Understand CQL:</strong> Cassandra Query Language (CQL) is used to interact with Cassandra’s data. It’s similar to SQL but tailored for Cassandra’s distributed environment.</li>
    <li><strong>Setting Up Keyspaces:</strong> Keyspaces are top-level namespaces for data replication and are essential in defining how data is stored across nodes.</li>
    <li><strong>Create Tables and Insert Data:</strong> Start with simple tables and experiment with data insertion, queries, and replication settings.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Conclusion</h2>
<p>Apache Cassandra is a powerful tool for managing large datasets in a distributed, scalable manner. Its architecture makes it highly suitable for applications requiring continuous availability and high performance.</p>
